<body>
    <h1>Training an Image Classifier with CreateML</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <div class=3D"contentLayout2">
<div class=3D"columnLayout fixed-width" data-layout=3D"fixed-width">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<h2 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-IntroductiontoImageClassifi=
cation"><strong>Introduction to Image Classification</strong></h2>
<p><strong>Image classification</strong> is a fundamental technique in comp=
uter vision that allows applications to identify and categorize the visual =
content in an image. Unlike object detection, which locates and identifies =
multiple elements within an image, or segmentation, which separates specifi=
c regions, classification assigns a single label representing the predomina=
nt category of the entire image. This capability is essential for creating =
intelligent experiences that quickly and efficiently understand visual cont=
ext.</p>
<p>In the Apple ecosystem, developing image classifiers is much easier than=
ks to a set of tools and frameworks such as CreateML, Core ML, and Vision. =
CreateML significantly simplifies the model training process. Once trained,=
 the model can be easily integrated into iOS, macOS, watchOS, and tvOS apps=
, taking advantage of Apple=E2=80=99s optimized hardware performance, inclu=
ding the CPU, GPU, and Neural Engine.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s explore, step by step, how to prepare your data, train an =
image classifier with CreateML, evaluate its performance, and integrate it =
into a real app. The goal is to provide a solid foundation so you, as an Ap=
ple Academy developer, can build intelligent applications that effectively =
understand images using Apple=E2=80=99s native technologies.</p>
<h2 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-GettingStarted:DefiningClas=
sesandDataset"><strong>Getting Started: Defining Classes and Dataset</stron=
g></h2><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper=
 confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image i=
mage-center" alt=3D"i1.png" width=3D"424" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"21254ccb0=
8bb8d184cd9037242083f9c4d6305b2cef6ceafd7cadbbd2940a964" data-image-src=3D"=
https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877328=
/i1.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692279254&amp;cacheVersion=
=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"2171" data-width=3D"1842" data-unresolved=
-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877367" data-linked-res=
ource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-re=
source-default-alias=3D"i1.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.a=
tlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-lin=
ked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877328" data-linked-resource-container-ver=
sion=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"6635d9cd-582d-4e2e-8185-4a2e24ed3870" data-medi=
a-type=3D"file" height=3D"500"></span>
<p>The starting point for training an effective image classifier is to clea=
rly define the classes (or categories) your model should recognize. These c=
lasses represent the different labels you want the model to assign to image=
s during inference. For example, if the goal is to classify three types of =
animals, your classes might be "Cat", "Dog", and "Koala", which will make u=
p our example dataset below.</p>
<p><strong>Choosing Categories (Labels)</strong></p>
<p>When selecting categories, it=E2=80=99s important to ensure that they ar=
e mutually exclusive and clearly defined to avoid ambiguities during traini=
ng. Think about the scope of your app and what really needs to be identifie=
d.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Avoid creating categories that are too similar and might confuse the mod=
el, unless you have a large and diverse dataset to distinguish them effecti=
vely.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-PreparingtheDataset">Prepar=
ing the Dataset</h2>
<p>In this step, we will download the images that we=E2=80=99ll use to trai=
n our model. Whenever we=E2=80=99re working with images, it=E2=80=99s impor=
tant to consider the file sizes. As mentioned in the =E2=80=9CTraining an O=
bject Detector=E2=80=9D section, large and heavy files can make the trainin=
g process costly and excessively long.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"columnLayout two-equal" data-layout=3D"two-equal">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<p>With that in mind, we initially divide our dataset into two required set=
s for model training: Training and Testing.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper conflu=
