<h3 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-UsingAugmentationstoImprove=
Training"><strong>Using Augmentations to Improve Training</strong></h3>
<p>One important feature CreateML offers during training is <strong>augment=
ations</strong> =E2=80=94 techniques that apply random transformations to t=
raining set images. These transformations artificially increase the diversi=
ty of your dataset, helping the model learn more robust and generalizable f=
eatures.</p>
<p>Some common augmentation options available in CreateML include:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Add Noise:</strong>&nbsp;Inserts small random pixel noise into t=
he image, making the model more resistant to variations in image quality.</=
p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Blur:</strong>&nbsp;Applies a blur filter, helping the model foc=
us on general features rather than highly specific details.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Crop:</strong>&nbsp;Randomly crops parts of the image, teaching =
the model to recognize objects in various framings.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Expose:</strong>&nbsp;Adjusts image brightness, making the model=
 more tolerant of lighting variations.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Flip:</strong> Mirrors the image horizontally or vertically, use=
ful when object orientation doesn=E2=80=99t change its class.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Rotate:</strong>&nbsp;Rotates the image by small random angles, =
helping the model recognize objects in different orientations.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-WhyUseAugmentations?"><stro=
ng>Why Use Augmentations?</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Increasing dataset size:</strong>&nbsp;Each original image gener=
ates multiple variations during training, effectively increasing the number=
 of examples without needing to collect more images.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Improving generalization:</strong>&nbsp;By exposing the model to=
 modified images, it learns to identify essential features and becomes less=
 sensitive to irrelevant factors like lighting or noise.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Reducing overfitting:</strong>&nbsp;Helps prevent the model from=
 memorizing the training examples exactly, promoting better performance on =
new, unseen data.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>In CreateML, simply enable the augmentation options, and these transform=
ations will be applied automatically and randomly during the training proce=
ss. It=E2=80=99s a good idea to experiment with different combinations to f=
ind the setup that best improves your classifier=E2=80=99s performance. Whe=
n you click the <strong>"Train"</strong> button, the process begins, and yo=
u can follow the progress through the iterations in the graphical interface=
:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper c=
onfluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image ima=
ge-center" alt=3D"ti4.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"0c841268cc=
21eef868118e45b864137b9c875433dd470a59f594cdb09daf0c22" data-image-src=3D"h=
ttps://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877328/=
ti4.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692278556&amp;cacheVersion=
=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"740" data-width=3D"1354" data-unresolved-=
comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877346" data-linked-reso=
urce-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-res=
ource-default-alias=3D"ti4.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.a=
tlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-lin=
ked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877328" data-linked-resource-container-ver=
sion=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"c33e94b9-12e2-4a65-b5bc-43821774176a" data-medi=
a-type=3D"file" height=3D"256"></span>
<p>Notice that the model converged early, well before the total number of i=
terations we configured (100). As shown in the CreateML interface, training=
 was completed after just 33 iterations, when the model reached a stable po=
int in terms of accuracy.</p>
<p>This early convergence indicates that the model had already learned enou=
gh patterns to classify the images accurately, avoiding unnecessary computa=
tional cost from further iterations that likely wouldn=E2=80=99t lead to si=
gnificant gains.</p>
<p>In the graph, we see that the <strong>training accuracy</strong> reached=
 <strong>94.9%</strong>, while the <strong>validation accuracy</strong> was=
 <strong>82%</strong>. This difference is common and reflects the model=E2=
=80=99s ability to generalize to new data not seen during training.</p>
<p>It=E2=80=99s important to monitor these indicators to ensure the model i=
sn=E2=80=99t overfitting =E2=80=94 when it fits too closely to the training=
 data and performs poorly on real-world data. If the gap between training a=
nd validation accuracy is too large, you may need to adjust parameters, inc=
rease dataset diversity, or apply additional techniques such as augmentatio=
ns.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>CreateML makes this visual monitoring easy and offers valuable insights =
so you can iteratively and efficiently fine-tune your model for the best im=
age classification performance.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>After training, the Evaluation tab presents a summary of the model=E2=80=
=99s performance:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-=
center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-em=
bedded-image image-center" alt=3D"ti5.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" s=
rc=3D"3a984efa91a309111963c2045c16a602131bfaa588f90bdcf02645c1be4f1520" dat=
a-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachm=
ents/1208877328/ti5.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692278556&am=
p;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"594" data-width=3D"676" dat=
a-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877343" dat=
a-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" da=
ta-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"ti5.png" data-base-url=3D"https://devel=
operacademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/=
png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877328" data-linked-resource-=
container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"0328499d-c577-4966-bc61-bf5d1dfdd1=
20" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"410"></span>
<p>In this tab, we can analyze metrics such as precision and recall, as wel=
l as view examples of correct and incorrect detections (false positives and=
 false negatives), helping us understand where the model is succeeding and =
where it's struggling.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-Exportingourmodel">Exportin=
g our model</h3>
<p>To export our model as a Core ML model file, go to the <strong>Output</s=
trong> tab and select one of the available options: <strong>Get</strong> or=
 <strong>Xcode</strong>. You can export the final result of your model or a=
 snapshot captured during the training stage.</p><span class=3D"confluence-=
embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"=
><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"ti6.png" widt=
h=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"38e5c28f2990727b819a23567e2be54d8ec16af70=
12c8e334cb7453649f1f2dc" data-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassi=
an.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877328/ti6.png?version=3D1&amp;modific=
ationDate=3D1750692278556&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D=
"740" data-width=3D"1015" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-r=
esource-id=3D"1208877340" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-re=
source-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"ti6.png" d=
ata-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-re=
source-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208=
877328" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"20ce9=
550-2170-4ff9-90ff-5cbebef8bc49" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"341"></=
span>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-IntegratingtheObjectDetecto=
rintoYourApp">Integrating the Object Detector into Your App</h3>
<p>Once you=E2=80=99ve completed the object detector, export it as a Core M=
L <code>.mlmodel</code> file and add it to your Xcode project.</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s an example of how to integrate your model using the Visio=
n framework:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import V=
ision
import CoreML

do {
    let model =3D try VNCoreMLModel(for: MyImageClassifierC5().model)
    let request =3D VNCoreMLRequest(model: model) { request, error in
        guard let results =3D request.results as? [VNClassificationObservat=
ion],
              let topResult =3D results.first else {
            return
        }
        print("Classification: \\(topResult.identifier) with certainty of \=
\(topResult.confidence)")
    }
    // ere you can create a VNImageRequestHandler to perform the request on=
 an image
} catch {
    print("Failed to load Core ML model: \\(error.localizedDescription)")
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Since this article is entirely focused on training, we won=E2=80=99t go =
into detail on how to deploy the trained model.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"TraininganImageClassifierwithCreateML-Conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>In this article, we learned how to train an image classifier using Creat=
eML, from dataset preparation to model training and evaluation. We also exp=
lored how to use <code>augmentations</code> to improve learning quality and=
 how to export the model to easily integrate it into an Apple app using the=
 Vision framework.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
